<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gortyn Code</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 450 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Great Code of Gortyn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gortyn Code is the longest surviving ancient Greek law code, inscribed in the Doric dialect of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> on a curved stone wall at Gortyn around 450 BCE. Its twelve columns, written in alternating directions, set out rules on marriage, divorce, inheritance, adoption, property and the status of slaves and serfs, giving a rare view of family law in a Greek city.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gortyn-code.jpg" alt="Gortyn Code" width="320" height="228" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Federico_Halbherr_Gortyn.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
